1/09/2026

Problem 1: Showing the correct crossing status
The Smart-Cross frontend needed to display whether a railway crossing was open, about to 
close, or closed. However, the frontend could not calculate this status reliably using train 
information alone.

Solution: We kept the crossing-status calculation in the backend and made the frontend display
the status returned by the server. This ensured that the timing logic remained in one place.



4/09/2026

Problem 2: Displaying train arrival and crossing closure times
The frontend needed to show useful information about the expected train arrival and the duration
for which the crossing might remain closed.

Solution: We structured the backend response to provide the required timing information, allowing 
the frontend to display the estimated arrival, warning, and closure details in a readable format.


11/09/2026

Problem 3: Handling changes in crossing information
The crossing status displayed on the frontend could become outdated when new train information 
became available.

Solution: We designed the frontend to obtain updated information through backend requests instead 
of relying only on previously displayed values. This allowed the interface to reflect newer data when 
it was retrieved successfully.

